---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import Evidence from '../../components/Evidence.astro';
import { essays } from '../../data/essays';
import { u } from '../../lib/url';
const essay = essays.find((e) => e.slug === 'paving-the-cloud')!;
const deps = u('docs/airframe/user/cloud-dependencies/');
const gaps = u('docs/airframe/admin/known-gaps/');
// The whole request for a managed database, as Airframe's ManagedPostgreSQL API takes it. The name is a Skyport app.
const request = `apiVersion: catalog.hangar.io/v1alpha1
kind: ManagedPostgreSQL
metadata:
  name: baggage-db
spec:
  size: small             # db.t4g.micro on AWS, B_Standard_B1ms on Azure
  version: "17"
  allowedCidrs:           # empty admits no one
    - 203.0.113.0/24
  protect: true           # deletion protection and a final snapshot`;
// The queue half of the same app: who needs it, and how, never which IAM actions.
const consumers = `  consumers:
    - awsLambdaTarget: baggage-api
      access: receive
      trigger: true`;
const evidence = [
  { text: 'MessageQueue, ObjectStorage and ManagedPostgreSQL in catalog.hangar.io, on AWS or Azure from one field', status: 'built' as const, href: deps },
  { text: 'Private, encrypted and TLS-only by construction, with no field to turn any of it off', status: 'built' as const, href: deps },
  { text: 'Lambda consumers get a grant on their own role, capped by the permissions boundary, never a key', status: 'built' as const, href: u('docs/airframe/admin/cloud-credentials/') },
  { text: 'Cloud targets that compose the function, the ECS service or the Container App, with keyless deploys', status: 'built' as const, href: u('docs/airframe/user/cloud-targets/') },
  { text: 'All three kinds verified live on AWS on October 24, 2026, teardown included', status: 'built' as const, href: u('library/airframe-cloud-dependencies/') },
  { text: 'The Azure versions run against a real subscription (known gap 10)', status: 'proposal' as const, href: gaps },
  { text: 'ECS services and Kubernetes apps as consumers, with their own cloud identity (known gap 8)', status: 'proposal' as const, href: gaps },
  { text: 'A private database network and a least-privilege app role (known gap 9)', status: 'proposal' as const, href: gaps },
  { text: 'Live health for cloud services in Tower', status: 'proposal' as const, href: u('docs/tower/cloud-deploys/') },
];
---
<Base title={essay.title} description={essay.dek} section="architecture">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="essay">
      <header class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow"><a href={u('architecture/')}>Architecture</a> · {essay.minutes} min read</p>
        <h1>Paving the <em>cloud</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">{essay.dek}</p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          On October 24, 2026, I sent one message to a queue and watched it fail twice. That was the plan. The queue was
          an Amazon SQS queue that Airframe had made a few minutes earlier, the function it triggered was running a
          placeholder image with no handler in it, and the queue was set to give up after two tries. Two failed runs,
          then the message turned up in the dead-letter queue, exactly where it should.
        </p>
        <p>
          What made me smile wasn't the failure. It was everything I hadn't written to get there. No IAM policy, no
          event source mapping, no redrive configuration, no encryption setting, no access key. I'd asked for a queue in
          a few lines of YAML and said which function should read from it. Then I deleted the file, and the queue, the
          bucket, the database, its network, its password and every grant went with it, with no snapshot left behind to
          pay for.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's the moment this essay is about. Airframe now offers a queue, object storage and a managed PostgreSQL
          database on AWS or Azure, beside the functions and cloud targets it learned in October. I think that's the
          point where a platform stops being a nicer way to talk to Kubernetes, and I want to say why.
        </p>

        <h2>More than a fancy Kubernetes front end</h2>
        <p>
          There's a fair criticism of platforms built on Crossplane, and I think it deserves a straight answer. If all your platform does
          is put a custom resource in front of a Helm chart, you've given developers YAML with extra steps. They're
          right to shrug at that. A wrapper around <code>kubectl</code> isn't a product. It's an inconvenience with a
          logo.
        </p>
        <p>
          What Kubernetes actually gives a platform is the machinery for keeping promises. A schema the API server
          enforces. Versions. Access control. A status that says how things are going. A controller that notices when
          the world has drifted from what was asked for, and puts it back. All of it driven from git, so every change is
          a reviewed commit. None of that machinery cares whether the thing being promised is a pod on my cluster or a
          database in a data centre far, far away.
        </p>
        <p>
          So in Hangar, Kubernetes is where the contract is kept. It was never meant to be where every road ends. A
          developer asks for a noun: a Python app, a cache, a queue, a database. Whether that noun becomes a pod or a
          managed service is the platform's business, and the request, the status and the rules look the same either
          way. Airframe has had a RabbitMQ broker and an in-cluster PostgreSQL for a while. It now has a
          <code>MessageQueue</code> and a <code>ManagedPostgreSQL</code> too, and they answer the same three questions:
          what can I ask for, what do I always get, and how do I know it worked.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Diagram id="paving/01-two-roads-one-contract" />

      <div class="prose">
        <p class="pull">Kubernetes is where the contract is kept, not where every road has to end.</p>

        <h2>What a paved road is made of, when it leads to a cloud</h2>
        <p>
          The fourth of my <a href={u('principles/')}>principles</a> is paved roads, with security built in. A golden
          path is good advice. A paved road goes further: the recommended way is also the easiest one, and the checks
          run whether anyone remembers them or not. For an app on the cluster, most of Hangar's road is one chart and one
          <code>cicd.yaml</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The cloud is a different kind of hard. Nobody struggles to click "create database" in a console. The hard part
          is everything around it: which network, which subnets, which security group, which parameter group, whether
          the storage is encrypted, whether plaintext connections are refused, where the password lives, who can reach
          it, and what happens to the data when the thing is deleted. Those are exactly the questions a developer doesn't
          want to learn on their first day, and that the security team is afraid they'll learn on their hundredth. Here's
          everything Skyport's baggage-api has to say to get a database:
        </p>
        <pre><code>{request}</code></pre>
        <p>
          Four fields, and none of them is a networking or security decision a developer has to get right. The road is
          everything the request doesn't have to say. I've come to think of it in five parts, and I'd use the same five
          to judge any platform's offer of a cloud resource, Hangar's included.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A contract</strong>, which is the short list of things you can ask for, in your own words: a size,
          a version, some storage, the networks allowed in. <strong>Defaults with no field</strong>, the things you
          always get. <strong>An identity</strong>, so the app can use the thing without anyone handling a key.
          <strong>An answer</strong>, a status a person and a script can both read. And <strong>a way out</strong>,
          because a road you can't leave cleanly is just a slower way to make a mess.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Diagram id="paving/02-what-a-road-is-made-of" />

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The missing field is the policy</h2>
        <p>
          In <a href={u('architecture/catalog-that-grows/')}>A service catalog that grows with the company</a> I wrote
          about where the architects' and security team's rules can live: in the schema, in the Composition, and in
          admission and CI. Back then the cloud resources were bring your own, so that was mostly a promise. Now there's
          something concrete to point at, and it taught me one thing I hadn't put into words.
        </p>
        <p>
          The strongest guardrail isn't a safe default. It's a field that doesn't exist. Plenty of infrastructure modules
          have a variable like <code>public = false</code>, and sooner or later somebody sets it to true for a
          good reason on a bad day. Airframe's <code>ObjectStorage</code> has no such field. All four of S3's public
          access blocks are on, ACLs are off, and there's no way to ask for anything else. A database admits no network at
          all until you list one. PostgreSQL refuses plaintext connections on both clouds. You can't misconfigure a
          setting you were never given.
        </p>
        <p>
          The flip side is that when something really is dangerous, the API makes you say it out loud. Azure deletes a
          storage account together with everything in it, so an Azure <code>ObjectStorage</code> isn't accepted unless it
          says <code>emptyOnDelete: true</code>. On AWS, a bucket that still has objects in it is kept unless you've said
          the same thing. I like that. The platform doesn't stop you doing the risky thing, it just won't let you do it
          by accident.
        </p>

        <h2>Identity is the real road</h2>
        <p>
          If I had to pick the one part of a cloud road that matters most, it's identity, and it's the part that's
          usually left as an exercise for the reader. The quickest way to let a function read a queue is an access key in
          an environment variable. It works on the first try, it works from anywhere, and it works for whoever copies it,
          for years.
        </p>
        <p>
          In Airframe, the developer says who needs the queue and how, and nothing about IAM:
        </p>
        <pre><code>{consumers}</code></pre>
        <p>
          From that, the composition finds the function, writes a policy onto that function's own execution role for this
          one queue and these actions, and connects the queue to it. Every role it touches carries the same permissions
          boundary, so no grant can ever be wider than the ceiling the security team set. The function's deploys
          already earn one-hour roles instead of stored keys, which I wrote up in
          <a href={u('library/keyless-cloud-deploys/')}>No stored AWS key for cloud deploys</a>. Put together, there is
          no AWS key anywhere in this picture. When I tested it, the IAM policy simulator allowed the function this queue
          and this bucket, and denied it every other.
        </p>
        <p>
          The part I care about is who that helps. The developer never had to learn IAM. The security team didn't have to
          review a hand-written policy, because the policy is the same every time and lives in a Composition they can
          read and approve once. Both of them got what they wanted from the same few lines.
        </p>

        <h2>One field picks the cloud, honestly</h2>
        <p>
          Each of the three kinds takes <code>spec.cloud</code>, <code>aws</code> or <code>azure</code>, fixed at
          creation. I want to be careful about what that does and doesn't mean, because "multi-cloud" is a phrase that has
          sold a lot of disappointment.
        </p>
        <p>
          It doesn't mean the clouds are the same. An SQS queue and a Service Bus queue are different things underneath,
          a Storage Account isn't an S3 bucket, and on Azure a queue's consumer still uses a connection string because
          the managed identity work isn't done. What it means is that the contract means the same thing on both: a
          private, encrypted, TLS-only resource, the same status, and the same two objects for the app to read, a
          <code>-connection</code> ConfigMap and, where one is needed, a <code>-credentials</code> Secret. Where a field
          only applies to one cloud, the XRD says so. I'd rather offer a little less on both than pretend.
        </p>
        <p>
          And honestly, the Azure versions have never run. They're render-tested and checked against the providers'
          schemas, but the providers aren't installed on my dev cluster yet. That's known gap 10, and it's on this page
          because it would be easy to leave off.
        </p>

        <h2>Why it has to be an API</h2>
        <p>
          A lot of internal developer portals put a nice form in front of a ticket queue. You fill in the form, somebody
          on another team picks up the ticket, and a few days later you have a database configured the way that person
          configures databases. That's a better experience than email, and it's still a queue in a nicer outfit. It
          can't be versioned, it can't be reviewed in a pull request, it can't be undone by deleting a file, and an agent
          can't use it.
        </p>
        <p>
          An API can be all of those. The third of my principles is an API-driven control plane ready for AI, and this
          is where it stops being abstract. Every cloud dependency reports one condition, <code>DependencyReady</code>,
          with a fixed set of reasons, and each reason has exactly one meaning. <code>ConsumerUnresolved</code> means the
          queue is fine but one of its consumers names a function that isn't there. <code>ManagedPostgreSQLFailing</code>
          carries the cloud provider's own error. A person reads that in Tower, a script branches on it, and an agent can
          fix the request and try again, all from the same field. The front door doesn't move either: a Backstage form,
          Tower, a pull request and an agent all create the same kind of resource, so adding three new kinds changed none
          of them.
        </p>
        <p>
          An IDP that's only a portal is a catalog of things you can request. An IDP with an API underneath is a catalog
          of things the platform will keep true. The second one is the one that scales past the platform team's
          inbox.
        </p>

        <h2>Three weeks of the wish list</h2>
        <p>
          The last essay ended with what I wanted next: let the XR make the cloud resource instead of bringing your own,
          give cloud services a real production approval, keep every role inside a boundary, and grow the menu by asking,
          maybe with a queue or a bucket. Looking back three weeks later, most of that list is built. The cloud targets
          compose the function, the ECS service and the Container App now. Cloud production gets a reviewed release pin,
          the same kind of approval a Kubernetes app has. The keys are gone. And the queue and the bucket arrived, with a
          database as well.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Diagram id="paving/03-three-weeks-of-the-wish-list" />

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Building it turned up three new gaps, and I'd rather name them than have you find them. Only a Lambda function
          can be a consumer today. An ECS service has no task role for the app's own code yet, and an app on the cluster
          has no cloud identity at all (gap 8). The database is reachable only over a public endpoint, because a Lambda
          function outside a VPC has no fixed address, so for now the password and TLS are doing more of the work than
          I'd like, and there's no separate least-privilege role for the app (gap 9). And Azure, as I said, hasn't run
          (gap 10). Tower still shows what was deployed to a cloud service but not whether it's healthy. Every one of
          these is in <a href={gaps}>Airframe's known gaps</a> with a proposed fix, and the full spec, with what was
          verified and how, is in the <a href={u('library/airframe-cloud-dependencies/')}>Library</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Gap 8 is the one I'm most looking forward to. Giving apps on the cluster their own cloud identity means a
          Python app in a pod can read the same queue a Lambda function does, with the same kind of grant and no key. At
          that point the line between a Kubernetes road and a cloud road mostly disappears, which is rather the point.
        </p>

        <h2>The road should be boring</h2>
        <p>
          The best compliment a platform can get is "I didn't have to think about it." Not that it's clever, or fast,
          or modern. Just that the thing they needed was there, it was safe, and they
          got on with their actual work. That's what I'm after with the cloud. A team that needs a queue should get one
          as easily as a team that needs a pod, with the same guardrails on and the same way out.
        </p>
        <p>
          Kubernetes will always be where Hangar keeps its promises. What it promises can be anywhere.
        </p>
        <p class="sign">James</p>
        <p class="more"><a href={u('architecture/')}>← All architecture essays</a></p>
      </div>

      <Evidence items={evidence} heading="Where Hangar does this, and where it doesn't yet" />
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
<style>
  .essay .hero .eyebrow a { color: inherit; }
  .essay .hero h1 { max-width: 22ch; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .prose pre {
    margin: 1.4rem 0; padding: 0.9rem 1rem; overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px;
    background: var(--raised); font-size: 0.84rem; line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .prose pre code { background: none; padding: 0; font-size: inherit; }
  .pull {
    font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.4vw + 1rem, 2rem); line-height: 1.25;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 0.2rem 0 0.2rem 1.2rem; margin: 2.5rem 0; max-width: 34ch;
  }
  .sign { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.4rem; margin-top: 2rem; }
  .more { margin-top: 2rem; }
</style>
